// Detector 3 of 4: orphaned resources — perfectly flat cost, nobody using them.
// Signal is coefficient of variation (stdev/mean). Anything in use fluctuates;
// an abandoned resource bills the identical amount every day forever.
//
// HONEST LIMITATION: 14 rows returned, 3 real. 21% precision, and it is not
// fixable by tightening the statistics — an abandoned managed disk and a
// healthy production VM are statistically identical. Both bill a fixed
// amount daily. The signal for "orphaned" is not in the billing data at all;
// it's in the resource graph (does a disk have a parent VM? is a public IP
// attached to a NIC?). That would need Azure Resource Graph joined back to cost.
//
// This produces a candidate list for human review, not a delete list.
// See 08b for a resource-type filter that reaches 100% precision by
// hardcoding which types are suspicious — a heuristic standing in for
// data we don't have.
//
// Finds: snapshot $305.49, managed disk $235.21, public IP $42.96
// ($583.66/yr) — alongside 11 legitimately-flat production resources.

AzureBilling_CL
| extend D = todatetime(Date)
| summarize DailyCost = sum(CostInBillingCurrency) by ResourceName, ResourceGroup, MeterCategory, D
| summarize Days = count(), Total = sum(DailyCost), StdDev = stdev(DailyCost), Avg = avg(DailyCost)
          by ResourceName, ResourceGroup, MeterCategory
| where Days > 300
| extend Variability = round(StdDev / Avg, 4)
| where Variability < 0.05
| project ResourceName, ResourceGroup, MeterCategory,
          AnnualCost = round(Total, 2), DailyAvg = round(Avg, 4), Variability
| order by AnnualCost desc
